firewall-bridge

nftables + netlink routing bridge for Linux. Python owns rules, state,
and presets; Rust is a kernel-only FFI surface.


v2.2.0 - 2026-11-03
-------------------

  nft runs as a child process in place of the libnftables link, and
  the bridge writes its table to the kernel in one nft transaction:
  on start and on each change to a started bridge's rules and
  groups. The C ABI and the Python API add to v2.1.8 and keep its
  signatures, and the database schema is the same; a consumer meets
  the change in what the library needs from its environment and in
  the input it accepts.

  Requirements (README, Requirements):

  * The nft program from the nftables package, at /usr/sbin/nft or
    at the absolute path in FIREWALL_BRIDGE_NFT_PATH. The .so has no
    link to libnftables.
  * CAP_NET_ADMIN that nft inherits: the loading process runs as
    root or holds it as an ambient capability. A capability given to
    the interpreter with setcap stays in the loading process.
  * SIGCHLD at its default disposition. The bridge waits for every
    nft it starts; with SIG_IGN the kernel reaps the child, and the
    call returns an error although the command was applied.
  * A writable /etc/iproute2. Routing table names go to the bridge's
    own file, /etc/iproute2/rt_tables.d/phantom.conf, and
    /etc/iproute2/rt_tables is left as it is.

  The table:

  * New C export nft_replace_rules, with the fw_nft_rule struct: one
    nft run adds, deletes and adds the table and its chains, then
    adds the rules, and returns their handles from nft's echo. When
    the kernel refuses a rule, the table is as it was.
  * start applies the enabled groups' rules with it, by group
    priority, then position and id. Adding a rule, removing one, and
    enabling, disabling or deleting a group rebuild the table the
    same way; stop replaces it with a table that holds no rules.
  * When the kernel refuses a rule, start removes what it applied
    and the bridge stays stopped, add_firewall_rule and
    add_routing_rule drop the refused row, and apply_preset and
    enable_group undo the group.
  * nft_init creates the table and its chains in one nft run, and
    start calls it first.
  * nft reads its command on stdin (-f -), so a command can be
    longer than the 128 KiB one argument holds. nft_add_rule and
    nft_remove_rule send nft JSON; an unknown action, an address of
    the other family or a bad prefix length returns InvalidParam.

  Input:

  * add_firewall_rule checks its fields before storing them, with
    the checks of a preset rule: an address is taken as well as a
    network, and a comment is limited to 128 bytes with no double
    quote, backslash or control character. A field that fails
    raises InvalidParamError.
  * A rule's addresses and its dnat target are of the rule's family.
  * A rule names tcp or udp together with a port, or neither; icmp
    is refused.
  * create_group raises GroupExistsError for a name that is taken,
    and the package exports GroupExistsError.
  * enable_group leaves a group that is enabled as it is.

  Routing:

  * rt_table_ensure refuses an empty name, a name with whitespace or
    of digits alone, and an entry longer than the 511 bytes of a
    line ip reads.
  * A policy rule or a route with an address of the other family is
    refused.
  * Policy and route rows use the table id of their group's ensure
    row.
  * Routes are added with link scope, the v4 default route too, and
    deleted with RT_SCOPE_NOWHERE, which takes a route of any scope.
    A delete with an empty device sends no device, protocol or type
    and matches destination and table; it is sent for a route in a
    table its group ensures, and a route row with no device in
    another table is not deleted.
  * While stopped, delete_group removes the group's routing rows
    from the kernel before the group is deleted, and
    remove_routing_rule removes its row whether applied or not.
  * flush_cache returns a failed write to
    /proc/sys/net/ipv4/route/flush as an error.

  Build and CI:

  * Rust 1.99.1 and edition 2024: every export is unsafe(no_mangle),
    and the exports that read caller pointers are unsafe extern "C"
    with a Safety section. The C symbols are those of v2.1.8 and
    nft_replace_rules.
  * Images on Rust 1.99.1 and Debian trixie, nftables and
    libnftables1 pinned to 1.1.3-1, the Python test dependencies in
    /opt/venv. The release .so is built in rust:1.99.1-trixie and
    requires GLIBC_2.39.
  * Clippy runs in the dev image with every warning an error
    (dev/clippy.sh).
  * Test and build run on every push to dev/firewall-bridge; publish
    runs on ubuntu-26.04.
  * kernel-matrix-firewall-bridge.yml runs the test steps in KVM
    guests of Ubuntu 22.04 GA (5.15), Debian 12.0 (6.1) and Debian
    13.0 (6.12) on a self-hosted runner, each step's output in the
    job log as it runs. Debian 11.0 (5.10) was run and is out of the
    matrix. README, Kernel Compatibility, has the table and the runs.

  84 Rust tests, the kernel tests among them in their own network
  and mount namespace; 312 Python unit tests; E2E in three stages,
  topology, rules and rules-caps (the daemon's capabilities, no
  network), the last two with 256 tests each that check nft, the
  database and the processes after every step. API impact:
  nft_replace_rules and fw_nft_rule in the C ABI, GroupExistsError
  in Python; input v2.1.8 accepted can be refused (Input, above). No
  schema impact.


v2.1.8 - 2026-10-15
-------------------

  Distribution moves from R2 to a tagged release, and the licence
  record moves from a hand-written file to cargo-about output. Both
  put what a consumer needs in order to check a package inside the
  package itself.

  * Publish creates vendor-firewall-bridge-v<VERSION> with both
    per-arch zips attached and the CHANGELOG linked from its notes.
    The rclone upload is gone; R2 keeps serving what is already
    published there. The job no longer needs the self-hosted runner
    or its credentials, so it runs on ubuntu-latest.
  * CHECKSUMS and its bot commit are gone with it. The zip carries
    libfirewall_bridge_linux.so.sha256 beside the library it
    describes, and the release records a digest for each asset.
  * No moving "latest" pointer: this repository holds several
    components and GitHub's /releases/latest answers for the
    repository, so consumers pin a tag.
  * about.toml states which licences are accepted; cargo-about
    refuses to generate anything for a crate carrying something
    else, so the policy and the document are one artifact.
    THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES is generated from it, for both target
    triples. The hand-written list had drifted — rusqlite and its
    six dependencies were listed although the tree no longer has
    them, ryu too, while serde_core and zmij were missing.
  * LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES ship inside each zip.


v2.1.7 - 2026-07-18
-------------------

  Hardens the FFI boundary itself. This crate lives inside the
  daemon's process: a Rust panic or a dangling read here does not
  fail an operation, it takes the whole VPN down. Two panics-in-
  waiting removed.

  * with_nft resolved the global context with expect(), so any nft_*
    call before nft_init — or after a failed init — was a guaranteed
    SIGABRT for the host. It now takes an uninit fallback and returns
    it with a clear last-error; the four call sites surface a new
    NotInitialized (-8) code, added to all three ABI surfaces
    (error.rs, header, Python NotInitializedError). The existing
    contract tests pin the agreement.
  * firewall_bridge_get_last_error returned a pointer into the
    mutex-held CString — freed the moment the next error is recorded,
    so a reader racing a writer dereferenced freed memory. New
    firewall_bridge_get_last_error_copy copies into a caller buffer
    entirely under the error lock (truncating to len-1 plus NUL,
    InvalidParam on bad arguments); the Python side reads through it.
    The raw variant stays for ABI compatibility, documented as
    deprecated in the header.

  26 Rust tests (2 new: uninit returns codes not panic; copy
  roundtrip, truncation and guards), 219 Python unit including the
  tri-surface contract tests, e2e against a live kernel. API impact:
  calls before nft_init now raise NotInitializedError instead of
  killing the process. No schema impact.


v2.1.6 - 2026-07-17
-------------------

  Makes PyYAML optional. presets.py imported yaml at module load, so
  `import firewall_bridge` raised ModuleNotFoundError on a host without
  it — even for a consumer that only ever passes resolved dicts, which
  is the daemon's whole pattern: service.py reads its own YAML and hands
  _load_spec a dict. yaml is needed only to parse a str/Path preset.

  * The yaml import moved into _load_spec, after the dict fast-path and
    only on the str/Path branch, with a clear message when it is
    missing. A dict preset now resolves with yaml uninstalled; a YAML
    string raises "PyYAML is required". The dependency is optional in
    fact, so the package behaves that way.
  * The publish preflight validates Cargo.lock too. It checked
    __init__.py against Cargo.toml but not the lock, so an out-of-sync
    firewall-bridge-linux [[package]] version would have passed — the
    one source bump.sh guards that CI did not. bump.sh's own comment
    claimed the workflow rejected mismatches; now it actually does.


v2.1.5 - 2026-07-17
-------------------

  Makes v2.1.4's DNAT work. It was added to the schema and announced
  as a first-class action, but it could never execute: ensure_table
  built no prerouting chain, and even with one, inet tables reject a
  bare `dnat to <addr>` — the table carries both families, so
  nftables cannot infer which one the address belongs to. The family
  field already held the answer. Presets keep declaring intent; the
  bridge now realises it for the table type.

  Alongside it, three surfaces that had no consumer and had rotted.

  * ensure_table creates the prerouting nat chain (dstnat priority).
  * dnat is emitted as `dnat ip to` / `dnat ip6 to`, driven by the
    rule's family. Verified against a live kernel.
  * Preset validation now covers the fields that build the nft
    command. in_iface, out_iface and state had no format check at
    all, and v2.1.4 widened action from enum to enum_prefix so
    `dnat to <addr>` would validate — which also admitted anything
    starting with "dnat ", and `flush ruleset` wipes every table on
    the host. Interfaces are allowlisted, state must be a subset of
    the ct states, and action is parsed structurally. Not reachable
    from the daemon, which fills these from constants, but this
    crate ships as a library whose stated job is to validate
    presets.
  * _is_cidr delegates to ipaddress. The IPv6 pattern was hex digits
    and colons with a 1-3 digit prefix, so ':::::/999' passed.
  * The C header still declared the pre-v2.1.2 routing signatures.
    family was threaded through src/lib.rs and _ffi.py and never
    reached the header, which ships in every release zip; ctypes
    does not read it, so nothing noticed. Corrected, and a contract
    test now asserts the header, the Rust exports and the ctypes
    bindings agree on names, arity and error codes.
  * requirements.txt compiled with --no-strip-extras. uv had reduced
    coverage[toml] to coverage, which pip treats as a separate key
    and rejects as unpinned under --require-hashes. The e2e image
    stopped building without a commit touching it — the base is the
    moving rust:1.85-bookworm tag, so a newer pip arrived
    underneath.


v2.1.4 - 2026-03-26
-------------------

  Adds DNAT as a first-class action in the preset schema. Presets
  can now express destination NAT rules alongside the existing
  filter and routing actions.


v2.1.3 - 2026-03-24
-------------------

  Schema patch riding on the v2.1.2 IPv6 work.

  * Added a migration for the routing_rules.family column so
    existing state databases gain dual-stack support without a
    manual reset.


v2.1.2 - 2026-03-24
-------------------

  Dual-stack release. IPv6 routing lands end-to-end and the test
  infrastructure is rebuilt around it on the same day.

  * Dual-stack IPv6 routing in the Rust FFI surface.
  * IPv6 routing support in the Python layer (mirrors the FFI
    additions).
  * Unit test runner rewritten on the Docker SDK; legacy test
    infrastructure removed in the same pass.
  * E2E test infrastructure introduced, orchestrated through
    compose-bridge.
  * CI split into parallel unit + E2E jobs (later refined to
    three parallel runners with the actions:read permission
    needed for workflow_call).
  * Vendor artifact hosting moved from Cloudflare Pages
    (vendor-artifacts.phantom.tc, served from the dev/vendor branch)
    to R2 direct upload, after the Pages setup ran into its service
    limits. Version is read directly from Cargo.toml at publish time.
  * Workflow push triggers ignore .github/ changes (no test/build
    runs for CI-only edits).
  * Added README and Turkish README with architecture diagrams.


v2.1.0 - 2026-03-02
-------------------

  Side-of-the-house reorganization. Realizes the thinning shape
  the wireguard-go-bridge moved toward in its own v2.1.

  Python takes ownership of rules, state, and presets — anything
  that benefits from being expressive and testable in user space.
  Rust is reduced to the kernel-only FFI surface: applying
  configuration to nftables and netlink, nothing more. Less code
  on the Rust side to keep aligned with kernel APIs, more
  flexibility on the Python side to evolve presets without
  touching FFI.

  Earlier within the v2.0 line:

    * Workflow split into separate test, build, and publish
      stages.
    * Publish target moved to dev/vendor as a flat directory with
      a VERSION file.
    * Fixed _ffi library discovery to look beside the package
      instead of in dist/ (matters once installed as a wheel).


v2.0.0 - 2026-03-01
-------------------

  SQLite state + ghost mode. Breaking change.

  v1.0 carried rule and routing state in-process. v2.0 moves
  state into SQLite so the bridge can be restarted, inspected,
  and reasoned about from outside the running daemon. Ghost mode
  enters as a first-class operating mode at the same time.

  * State persistence rewritten on SQLite (replaces the v1.0
    in-process state).
  * Ghost mode added as a first-class operating mode.
  * Removed the redundant test job from the package workflow.


v1.0.0 - 2026-02-28
-------------------

  Initial release. Reorganized in v2.0; documented here for
  historical continuity.

  First cut of the firewall bridge: nftables for filter and NAT,
  netlink for routing. State held in-process.

  * nftables + netlink routing bridge.
  * Mocked _resolve_platform in discovery tests to avoid false
    matches against dist/.
  * LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES inventory.
